I have a complete picture. Here are my findings.

## Summary

The **hard capacity limit of 50 owners/city is fully implemented** (enforcement + 409 escalation), but the **`capacityWarning` field expected by the test does NOT exist yet** in production code or the OpenAPI schema. A grep for `capacityWarning` across `src/main` returns zero hits — it appears only in the test `AcceptanceTest.java`. That test would currently fail.

---

## 1. Capacity enforcement (the "50 per city" check)

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/EnsureCityCapacity.java`** — the enforcement function class:

```java
public class EnsureCityCapacity {

    private static final int CAPACITY = 50;

    public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)
            throws CityAtCapacityException {
        String city = request.getCity();
        long inCity = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
            .filter(owner -> city != null && city.equalsIgnoreCase(owner.getCity()))
            .count();
        if (inCity >= CAPACITY) {
            throw new CityAtCapacityException(city);
        }
    }
}
```

Case-insensitive city match; rejects the 51st create with a 409.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/CityAtCapacityException.java`** — checked exception, message `"City is at capacity: " + city`.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** — responds `409 CONFLICT` with empty body (`ResponseEntity<Void>`).

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.CityAtCapacityException.yml`** — wires the exception to the handler:
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler
```

---

## 2. The POST-owners YAML pipeline

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`** — full pipeline (authorize `OWNER_ADMIN`, all steps under `transaction`). Order:

`check` (CheckIdempotency) → `validate` (ValidateOwnerFields) → `validatePostcode` → `normalizeTelephone` → `normalizeEmail` → `rejectDisposableEmail` → `normalizeAddress` → `ensureUniqueIdentity` (EnsureUniqueIdentity) → **`ensureCityCapacity` (EnsureCityCapacity)** → `build` (BuildOwner) → `ensureDailyLimit` → `assignCustomerCode` → `deduplicateCustomerCode` → `assignNamesakeCount` → `save` (SaveOwner) → `record` (RecordIdempotency) → `logAudit` (LogAuditOnCreate) → `respond` (RespondWithOwnerCreated).

`ensureCityCapacity` runs after unique-identity and before `build`, using the `OwnerFieldsDto` request (not the built Owner).

---

## 3. Response DTO and responders

The create/fetch response type is **`OwnerDto`** (generated at build time from `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`, schema `Owner` at lines 2089–2255 — there is **no** hand-written `OwnerDto.java`). Both responders build it identically via `OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto` then decorate it:

**`.../rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** (POST responder, returns `ResponseEntity<OwnerDto>`, `201 Created`, `Location: /api/owners/{id}`):
```java
OwnerDto dto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
dto.setBulkSignupWarning(BulkSignup.warned(owner, ownerRepository));
PossibleDuplicate.mark(dto, owner, ownerRepository);
Membership.mark(dto, owner, ownerRepository);
response.send(ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/owners/" + owner.getId())).body(dto));
```

**`.../rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwner.java`** (GET `/api/owners/{ownerId}` responder — this is the path the capacity-warning test hits via `getOwner(id)`):
```java
OwnerDto dto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
dto.setBulkSignupWarning(BulkSignup.warned(owner, ownerRepository));
PossibleDuplicate.mark(dto, owner, ownerRepository);
Membership.mark(dto, owner, ownerRepository);
response.send(dto);
```
GET pipeline: **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners/{ownerId}.GET.yml`** — `load` (LoadOwner) → `respond` (RespondWithOwner), under `readonly-transaction`.

### OwnerDto (schema `Owner`, openapi.yml lines 2089–2255)
Composed as `allOf [OwnerFields + these read-only fields]`: `id`, `displayName`, `salutation`, `initials`, `customerCode`, `checkDigit`, `householdId`, `identityKey`, `namesakeCount`, `membershipNumber`, `fiscalYear`, `membershipPoints`, `membershipLevel`, `locality`, `timezone`, **`bulkSignupWarning`** (boolean), `possibleDuplicate` (boolean), `possibleDuplicateOf` (int), `contactPreference`, `telephoneDisplay`, `ageBand`, `selfLink`, `deleted`, `pets` (required). **There is NO `capacityWarning` property.**

The request DTO used by the pipeline/check is `OwnerFieldsDto` (from schema `OwnerFields`, referenced via `getCity()`).

---

## 4. The warning-decorator pattern (template for a would-be `capacityWarning`)

The closest existing analog to a "warning" boolean is **`.../rest/function/owner/BulkSignup.java`**:
```java
public final class BulkSignup {
    private static final int WARN_THRESHOLD = 80;
    public static boolean warned(Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        LocalDate day = owner.getRegistrationDate();
        long registeredThatDay = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
            .filter(existing -> day.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))
            .count();
        return registeredThatDay > WARN_THRESHOLD;
    }
}
```
Sibling response-time decorators with the same shape: `Membership.java` (sets `membershipPoints`/`membershipLevel`) and `PossibleDuplicate.java` (sets `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf`). All three are stateless helpers invoked from both responders. A `capacityWarning` feature would most naturally be a new helper of this form plus an `openapi.yml` field plus a `dto.setCapacityWarning(...)` call in both responders.

---

## 5. Tests related to owner capacity

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** — the only capacity test, and it targets the **unimplemented** warning:
```java
/** capacity-warning: 'capacityWarning' is true when the owner's city already holds 40-49
 * owners (approaching the 50 hard limit). Fill a fresh city to 40, then the next owner in it warns. */
@Test
void coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity() throws Exception {
    String city = "Warn" + seq();
    fillCity(city, 40);
    ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
    o.put("city", city);
    int id = createOwnerOk(o);
    getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.capacityWarning").value(true));
}
```
Expected semantics: `capacityWarning == true` when the city already holds **40–49** owners (approaching the 50 hard cap).

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`** — helpers: `fillCity(String city, int n)` (lines 170–179) creates `n` unique owners in one city to drive the per-city rules; `createOwnerOk` (160–162), `getOwner` (189–191), plus `ownerNode()`/`seq()`. No test currently exercises the 50-owner 409 rejection directly.

### Key gap
`capacityWarning` is referenced only by the test. It is absent from `openapi.yml` (so `OwnerDto` has no `setCapacityWarning`), and neither responder nor any helper sets it. Implementing it requires: add the property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`, add a helper (BulkSignup-style, threshold 40, same case-insensitive city count as `EnsureCityCapacity`), and call it from `RespondWithOwner` and `RespondWithOwnerCreated`.

