Composed as `allOf [OwnerFields + these read-only fields]`: `id`, `displayName`, `salutation`, `initials`, `customerCode`, `checkDigit`, `householdId`, `identityKey`, `namesakeCount`, `membershipNumber`, `fiscalYear`, `membershipPoints`, `membershipLevel`, `locality`, `timezone`, **`bulkSignupWarning`** (boolean), `possibleDuplicate` (boolean), `possibleDuplicateOf` (int), `contactPreference`, `telephoneDisplay`, `ageBand`, `selfLink`, `deleted`, `pets` (required). **There is NO `capacityWarning` property.**

The request DTO used by the pipeline/check is `OwnerFieldsDto` (from schema `OwnerFields`, referenced via `getCity()`).

---

## 4. The warning-decorator pattern (template for a would-be `capacityWarning`)

The closest existing analog to a "warning" boolean is **`.../rest/function/owner/BulkSignup.java`**:
```java
public final class BulkSignup {
    private static final int WARN_THRESHOLD = 80;
    public static boolean warned(Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        LocalDate day = owner.getRegistrationDate();
        long registeredThatDay = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
            .filter(existing -> day.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))
            .count();
        return registeredThatDay > WARN_THRESHOLD;
    }
}
```
Sibling response-time decorators with the same shape: `Membership.java` (sets `membershipPoints`/`membershipLevel`) and `PossibleDuplicate.java` (sets `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf`). All three are stateless helpers invoked from both responders. A `capacityWarning` feature would most naturally be a new helper of this form plus an `openapi.yml` field plus a `dto.setCapacityWarning(...)` call in both responders.

---

## 5. Tests related to owner capacity

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** — the only capacity test, and it targets the **unimplemented** warning:
```java
/** capacity-warning: 'capacityWarning' is true when the owner's city already holds 40-49
 * owners (approaching the 50 hard limit). Fill a fresh city to 40, then the next owner in it warns. */
@Test
void coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity() throws Exception {
    String city = "Warn" + seq();
    fillCity(city, 40);
    ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
    o.put("city", city);
    int id = createOwnerOk(o);
    getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.capacityWarning").value(true));
}
```
Expected semantics: `capacityWarning == true` when the city already holds **40–49** owners (approaching the 50 hard cap).

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`** — helpers: `fillCity(String city, int n)` (lines 170–179) creates `n` unique owners in one city to drive the per-city rules; `createOwnerOk` (160–162), `getOwner` (189–191), plus `ownerNode()`/`seq()`. No test currently exercises the 50-owner 409 rejection directly.

### Key gap
`capacityWarning` is referenced only by the test. It is absent from `openapi.yml` (so `OwnerDto` has no `setCapacityWarning`), and neither responder nor any helper sets it. Implementing it requires: add the property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`, add a helper (BulkSignup-style, threshold 40, same case-insensitive city count as `EnsureCityCapacity`), and call it from `RespondWithOwner` and `RespondWithOwnerCreated`.

